ur plantings they  would be a couple meters away  from our experimental planting maybe  there's a laneway that's going in  between  the field which is also really helpful  for getting cultivation or harvesting  equipment in and out of the field as  well  thank you  and i see a couple folks in the chat are  saying that um  they see wild bumblebees on their farm  which makes me happy and sea square is  confirming that wild bees are better  than bumble bees  is that correct  um so in the case of strawberry  pollination that is correct we don't see  so there are queen bumblebees that are  flying around during strawberry bloom i  occasionally see a wild queen so you can  tell they're wild because they're really  big whereas the workers in the colonies  that you buy are much smaller compared  to the wild queens that are out they  don't tend to visit strawberry it's  mostly this other suite of really  diverse and much smaller pollinators  that are visiting strawberry and  providing services  so would these be like those tiny  uh shiny green ones exactly yeah so some  of the pictures i showed earlier  um show you just the absolutely gorgeous  diversity  of different bees that we've been seeing  in our surveys so shiny metallic green  midnight blue  red with stripes they're they're very  beautiful  that's incredible  um oh great a question from brian young  um  he says that oh it's a comet we stopped  using quads five or so years ago and we  see many more wild bees carpenter bees  small bees  that's fantastic  yeah and we've actually one thing that  was wild to see in those bumblebee  colonies um the reason why wild queens  are going in there is because it's part  of their natural behavior to try to  usurp or to steal the colonies of other  wild queens that have already  established they basically get all the  benefits of having a nest without having  to do all the work of starting that nest  and we see other bees as well who are  also trying to get in so other bumblebee  sp