ve also explored other options  that growers can use like planting some  nice floral habitats on their farm  and those can provide nesting resources  of course pollen and nectar  refuge from pesticide applications or in  the case of natural enemies that might  be attacking pests it gives them some  alternative resources to use at periods  when pest populations might be low  within the crop  so we established  two  plots on 12 different farms across the  region one of the plots was just a  control planting and another one you can  see a little bit from the satellite  image this dark green patch here that's  a native perennial wildflower planting  that we put in right next to our  experimental strawberry plantings that's  what one of those plantings looks like  when it's sort of in its full glory  and what we found when we looked at bee  visitation comparing within each farm  the difference in bees or in some cases  we also looked at pests and yield  comparing this plot that had a  wildflower planting to the one that did  not  is this interesting hump-shaped curve so  here above the line means that the  wildflower planting had more bees and  actually below the dotted line means  that it had less bees so we see this  kind of goldilocks a zone with moderate  amounts of natural habitat in the  surrounding landscape predicting farms  that will do the best in terms of  increasing pollination services when  they add a wildflower strip so some  benefit but only at this sort of sweet  spot of farms and very likely that's  because in farms with very little  natural habitat there's just not a  sufficient pollinator population and to  recruit to that relatively small  wildflower planting  and at farms where there's a lot of  natural habitat adding a small  wildflower planting is a drop in the  bucket they already had sufficient  pollination services  to begin with we saw um that even at  those farms where there was a positive  effect it took about three years for us  to start to see that effect so  definitely a long-term investment and  something to think about in terms of how  you're rotating  your plantings if you're establishing  one of these  wildflower habitats that's going to take  three years before you start to see an  effect  in terms of pests we actually do see  evidence that there can be a spillover  of pests so there was sort of a  goldilocks zone again here in the middle  but no area where there was truly a  substantial benefit of adding a  wildflower planting in terms of a  reduction in pest pressure  and in some of these areas here we  actually saw  substantial increases in pest pressure  at those sites some of that  has to do with the fact that our key  pests like the tarnish plant bug  really like  many of the plants that showed up so  many of the plants here with asterisks  are ones that we planted but some of  them for example like a ridgeron that  daisy fleabane  that we saw earlier those  uh volunteered within our wildflower  plantings and they supported a lot of  tarnished plant bug but not a whole lot  of bees were using those compared to  some of the other species that we  intentionally planted into our plantings  it also was a little variable depending  on what our herbivore taxa was so  tarnished plant bugs which were the main  thing causing um damage in terms of  insects to our crop we did see a little  bit of an increase in tarnish plant bugs  at sites that had a wildflower strip  compared to controls  but  other pests like strawberry sap beetle  and other herbivores like aphids or  spittle bugs tended to be a little bit  lower and that's work that um one of the  undergraduates who worked with us  through the summer scholars program  ellie mccabe who's pictured here  spearheaded that project  so overall takeaways um from today are  that we are seeing um substantial  variation in weed pressure across farms  using some of those physical barriers  like plastic mulches or weed fabrics can  be effective although  there are certainly costs and things to  think about in terms of how you manage  those across their life cycle  grasses tend to be one of the most  impactful  weeds within the system but there are  other flowering plants that may be  providing some beneficial services in  terms of supporting pollinators or  natural enemies  overall in terms of pollination those  wild bees are really important for  providing pollination services on farms  and honeybees  and even  managed bumblebee colonies which are  quite expensive to purchase are probably  not going to be very effective  pollinators and if anything they're just  out there pollinating your flowering  weed populations  and in terms of pest management  wildflower plantings can if uh not very  carefully managed result in spillover of  pests so it's really important to make  sure that the seed mixes that you're  getting or even the volunteer plants  like fleabane are not  coming up and providing these really  nice alternative hosts for  potential pests within the system  so i'm incredibly grateful to all of the  growers some of which are pictured here  many of whom i think are on the call  today who allowed me over  many years to come and collect some of  this data on your farms i also have to  thank all of the labs that i've  collaborated with over the years and  have contributed to the project of  our funders as well and if there's time  i think we're near the end but i'm happy  to take any questions  laura we can't hear you  uh no i'm not sure what's gone wrong  hi i'll um  laura  i'm not sure what's going on but i can  take over uh we do have some time for  questions session 4 is going to start at  3 45 pm  uh so thank you so much heather for this  talk this was super interesting  um  and  jim is asking  how might you convince growers to stop  using bumblebee quads for pollination  because money  oh go ahead yeah um so i would say of  course if you see on your own farm if  you're  purchasing these and they are working  for you  that's great  one piece of data that i think was  really really impactful for me which i  didn't share earlier  is this one so we worked in  not just strawberry for the study but we  also surveyed in raspberry and tomato  and we saw very few bumble bees so there  was no bumble bees in our control plots  very few bumblebees visiting strawberry  across all the farms we surveyed that  had these quads  um and importantly in later flowering  crops especially for tomato we actually  saw that there were fewer bumblebees at  states where we had put a quad compared  to sites that don't and the main reason  for that just some work spearheaded by  another student i worked with olivia  miller is that these quads are killing  large numbers of wild bumblebee queens  that enter through these um hive  entrances when they do not have  queen excluders on them  i have a little video of that you can  see there's  queens that are wild that's a wild queen  trying to get into one of these quads if  you do not have an excluder so there's  an excluder on there that keeps her from  getting in you can easily kill 20 or 30  wild bumblebee queens and that's a  colony then that no longer establishes  and produces workers that pollinate all  of your other crops for the rest of the  season so that's one piece of data that  really convinced me  why it's probably not a good idea to  invest in these even if they were  increasing your pollination in  strawberry  oh that just breaks my heart thank you  so much for sharing that video  um i also i do need to remind everyone  that if you want dec credits  you need to fill out the survey that's  in the chat one more time we will not be  offering the ec credits in the fourth  session so this is your last chance  um  and oh oh here's a good question um  chuck is asking they see a lot of  bumblebees pollinating and blueberries  what do you think about that  so blueberries could actually be a  crop system where putting quads in  may be effective i've definitely seen a  lot of bumblebees as well pollinating  blueberries  they're very effective blueberry  pollinators because bumblebees do that  buzz pollination activity so most of the  pollination needs for blueberry as long  as there's sufficient natural habitat  around can be met by the wild bee  populations there's some great work out  of university of vermont that's showing  that supplementing with bumblebees only  very rarely  gives a positive impact although more so  than it does for strawberry but i would  still be quite worried about these  long-term population impacts of putting  these quads out  particularly when they do not have a  excluder on them which is not currently  how companies you'd have to ask  especially to get those excluders  another question that i have for you is  one time i was at a talk  um and this entomologist researcher says  that  on farms that spray pesticides and  insecticides  and they have um  they have these field borders  of wildflowers they actually found the  highest concentrations of pesticides in  those  borders which of course isn't  it's a little bit counterintuitive  so i'm wondering  where  do you think would be a good place to  uh encourage natural vegetation  in a farm system  yeah i think that's a really tricky  question i also get that a lot from  folks who are doing a lot of under  canopy management so blueberry growers  and also orchard folks who want to  figure out like if i'm spraying should i  mow down all of the dandelions and  clovers that are in my field and i think  that is a good practice to do if you're  spraying anything that has high toxicity  for beneficial insects  because they can always forage elsewhere  to find some of those pollen and nectar  resources they need but if they forage  on something toxic and they die then  you know you're out of luck in terms of  your pollinators  um  not having  your planting right immediately at the  edge but with some buffer is probably a  good idea so often in our plantings they  would be a couple meters away  from our experimental planting maybe  there's a laneway that's going in  between  the field which is also really helpful  for getting cultivation or harvesting  equipment in and out of the field as  well  thank you  and i see a couple folks in the chat are  saying that um  they see wild bumblebees on their farm  which makes me happy and sea square is  confirming that wild bees are better  than bumble bees  is that correct  um so in the case of strawberry  pollination that is correct we don't see  so there are queen bumblebees that are  flying around during strawberry bloom i  occasionally see a wild queen so you can  tell they're wild because they're really  big whereas the workers in the colonies  that you buy are much smaller compared  to the wild queens that are out they  don't tend to visit strawberry it's  mostly this other suite of really  diverse and much smaller pollinators  that are visiting strawberry and  providing services  so would these be like those tiny  uh shiny green ones exactly yeah so some  of the pictures i showed earlier  um show you just the absolutely gorgeous  diversity  of different bees that we've been seeing  in our surveys so shiny metallic green  midnight blue  red with stripes they're they're very  beautiful  that's incredible  um oh great a question from brian young  um  he says that oh it's a comet we stopped  using quads five or so years ago and we  see many more wild bees carpenter bees  small bees  that's fantastic  yeah and we've actually one thing that  was wild to see in those bumblebee  colonies um the reason why wild queens  are going in there is because it's part  of their natural behavior to try to  usurp or to steal the colonies of other  wild queens that have already  established they basically get all the  benefits of having a nest without having  to do all the work of starting that nest  and we see other bees as well who are  also trying to get in so other bumblebee  sp