ment styles you can see the  diversity of different  systems that we were working in but all  within  june bearing strawberries so matted row  systems in june berry is still what  we're seeing is the most common  production style but one trend that i've  noticed is even within june bearing  strawberry production i'm seeing a lot  more  of the plastic mulch for weed  suppression and to help keep plants and  fruits off the soil so seeing that used  not just within the rows but also seeing  a lot of the weed suppression fabric  between rows and even some growers that  have been experimenting with using  living mulches between the rows so  there's an example here and the left of  a grower that was using  a living mulch that was then terminated  just before flowering began in the  spring so i'm going to start off with  talking a little bit about the  distribution of weed pressure that we  saw across the farms that we surveyed so  in this plot every farm has two points  that are connected with a line and what  we saw is that grasses were generally  the most problematic weeds on farms in  some cases making up more than 40  percent of the cover within these  randomized meter squared quadrats where  we did  our sampling but we were also seeing in  many cases  some problematic forbs as well showing  up in plantings  many growers did a great job of keeping  their weed pressure  quite low and of course a lot of the  farms that were using  these  intensive weed suppression systems using  black plastic and using weed fabrics did  a good job at keeping weeds low although  only around 20  of the farms that we surveyed were using  this plastic mulch and we did certainly  see examples of growers who were using  matted rose systems that were still able  to keep weed pressure quite low  in this case through pretty intensive  hand weeding practices with uh big crews  that were going out and suppressing  weeds or through  pretty regular use of herbicides or  pre-emergent herbicides  we also noticed although we tried to  standardize the age of fields that we  were sampling within that some of those  um fields that had been in production  for several years of course were more  weedy than those that had been more  recently established  as part of our broader surveying efforts  on farms  looking at weed pressure was just one  small component we also looked at  fruit production and part of that survey  work was using these sentinel potted  plants that we put out on farms so  unsurprisingly farms that had really  high weed pressure had lower overall  production  but by using these sentinel potted  plants we remove that competition factor  that weeds have and we also in some  cases were able to minimize some of the  issues of microclimate that's created  when there's heavy weed pressure in a  field so that we could see what some of  the effects of those in-field weeds are  mediated through other routes of action  so in that case  by  influencing pest pressure dynamics  within the crop  or by influencing natural enemies or  even beneficial insects like pollinators  that may all also influence yield in the  crop  and curiously what we found is actually  that some of our farms that had the  highest weed pressure  had lower  damage in their fruit and higher average  fruit weight so higher quality berries  produced at these sites that had higher  levels of weed pressure again that's  just in the sentinel plants where we've  removed the competition effects so  clearly there would be  strong effects on food production in the  field themselves  but when we look at some of the  most common needs that we found so again  grasses which are really hard to  distinguish among species we saw of  course  crab grasses and also some nutsedges  that were problematic in some areas but  many of the other weeds that are forbs  were some plants that provide floral  resources potentially for  beneficial insects so things like purple  dead nettle or  yellow rocket fleabane especially in  field margins was a common one  and we know that there are many insects  that are very important for strawberry  production systems that can be found in  close association with many of these  different weedy species so  one example here that you're seeing  across almost every  weedy plant species is that brown plant  bug the tarnished plant bug here  but also some beneficials are associated  with many of these as well so many  different  native wild pollinator species that  could be promoting  yield and pollination on farms  some previous work that we did across  farms in the region in  2012  found that tarnished plant bugs are  causing as much as 40 percent damage to  berries across the farms that we  surveyed but fortunately there is a  complex of natural enemies  in a genus of wasps that include some  species that are both native as well as  some that were introduced by the usda  that do provide fairly good control  of tarnished plant bug populations  particularly when there are floral  resources around and natural habitats  that can support  the that natural enemy population  when we're looking at fruits  um many people  are  able to distinguish between when  a malformation on a fruit or a cat  facing  is likely to have been caused by the  feeding damage of plant bugs versus by  poor pollination damage but i will say  these frequently can look very similar i  have seen  and tried to score for damage hundreds  of thousands of strawberry fruits and  i in this case know confidently because  these were experimentally exposed to  either poor pollination or to an  herbivore but there's also very many  cases especially in particular cultivars  or when tarnished plant bug feeding  happens early that it's very challenging  to distinguish so the overall sort of  guidelines are that when you have  large well-developed seeds that are all  clustered together that's a very likely  to be symptomatic of tarnish plant bug  feeding  whereas a similar kind of damage but  having very small seeds is more likely  to be poor pollination damage but it's  really hard to distinguish so if you see  these kinds of damage  on your fruit it's good to think about  what the insects are that you were  seeing in the field  during flowering and just after  flowering  we also know that pollination is pretty  important for strawberry not equally  important across all cultivars but this  is data that we took from  joule which at the time of our survey  was the most common cultivar grown  across farms and likely is still quite  common within the region we did a couple  pollination treatments on all of the  fruit across the farms that we surveyed  doing bagging so that flowers were only  able to self-pollinate by wind or  by their own pollen  some open pollination where flowers were  exposed just to the pollinators that  were available at that farm and then  supplemental hand pollination so we can  see what the maximum size of fruit would  be if pollen was not limited at the site  and we do see about a 45  increase in yields comparing the self to  hand pollination but we also see that  the open pollination is not as high as  hand pollination is which indicates to  us that while  the insects that are on a farm are  providing some good pollination services  it's not always enough to meet the needs  of the  plantings across farms and so if we  break this down so this is every farm  that we surveyed where their data is an  individual bar  and we're just simply subtracting the  weight of the fruits that were produced  by the supplemental hand pollination to  the weight of produce of fruits that was  produced just through the action of  pollinators on their farm we see that  between 30 and 40 percent of farms have  this pollen limitation where there is a  pollination need that's not being met  and that's generally explained by the  number of insects that we saw  particularly wild bees that were  on the flowers during the bloom period  so if we look at the hand pollinated  fruits it doesn't really matter how many  bees we saw on a farm in our  standardized timed visitation surveys  but what we saw for  the open pollinated is that if farms had  a lot of  bees that were visiting their flowers we  didn't see much difference at all  between the open pollinated and our hand  pollinated treatment but we saw the  biggest difference at farms that had  very few visitors during our timed  transect surveys during the early bloom  period  the community of bees that's visiting  strawberry in new york is not only  incredibly beautiful but also really  diverse and interestingly  not nominated by the european honeybee  so on average across all of our sites we  have only about seven percent of visits  coming from european honeybees i think  this is  a characteristic of the diversity that  we have across all of our farming  systems within the region but i've also  heard data from colleagues across the  northeast that they see a similar  pattern in other fruit crops as well  so one question i get a lot is among all  this diversity of bees who seems to be  doing the best job  and uh there was a recent study that i  think is really intriguing from our  neighbors to the north in canada  that showed that on a per visit basis  wild bees are more effective than honey  bees are and it actually takes about  three visits by a honey bee in order for  them to start to become equal in the  pollination services that they are  providing  from wild bees  and when we have a mixed community as  would sort of naturally occur when  you're not excluding visits from one  type or another one really intriguing  result that they found is that even when  there were multiple different bee  species visiting a strawberry flower it  mattered who visited first and in an  interesting way where flowers that were  visited first by a honeybee  still had lower yields independent of  the number of total visits compared to  flowers that were visited first by a  wild bee it's not clear  why exactly that is but kind of an  interesting pattern that overall  suggests that wild bees are a really  important part of the pollination  services that are provided to strawberry  and unfortunately one of the trends that  we have seen is  that sites that have more agricultural  cover in the area surrounding a farm  tend to have the fewest number of wild  bee visits on the farm so what can  growers do if they find themselves in  this spectrum of  more simplified landscape compared to  those that have a higher cover of  natural habitats like wood lots or old  fields surrounding their farm uh one  option we looked at more recently was  using these um managed bumblebee quads  so we know honeybees maybe aren't the  most effective how about this other  managed pollinator like bumblebees that  you can bring and supplement onto the  farm we did a large survey in both um  2018 and 2019 looking at the  effectiveness of bumblebees  and we found that  um although we still do see evidence  that there's pollen limitation we do not  see  any difference in the magnitude of  change between our open pollination and  our hand pollination treatment at sites  that did not get a bumblebee  quad versus ones that did get bumblebee  quads applied to them so  the bumblebees were not very effective  at meeting that pollination need that  would have normally been filled by wild  bees  and probably a big reason for that is  that we really didn't see very many  bumblebees visiting the strawberry when  we looked at the pollen that those  bumblebee colonies brought back into  their hive we actually found on average  only about four percent of the pollen  that they were returning with was  rosaceas pollen so that's the group that  includes strawberry  and about 95 of it was other pollens  that were predominantly  weeds so they were doing a good job of  visiting weeds but not so much at  visiting the strawberry plantings  themselves so if managed bees are not a  great solution  um we've also explored other options  that growers can use like planting some  nice floral habitats on their farm  and those can provide nesting resources  of course pollen and nectar  refuge from pesticide applications or in  the case of natural enemies that might  be attacking pests it gives them some  alternative resources to use at periods  when pest populations might be low  within the crop  so we established  two  plots on 12 different farms across the  region one of the plots was just a  control planting and another one you can  see a little bit from the satellite  image this dark green patch here that's  a native perennial wildflower planting  that we put in right next to our  experimental strawberry plantings that's  what one of those plantings looks like  when it's sort of in its full glory  and what we found when we looked at bee  visitation comparing within each farm  the difference in bees or in some cases  we also looked at pests and yield  comparing this plot that had a  wildflower planting to the one that did  not  is this interesting hump-shaped curve so  here above the line means that the  wildflower planting had more bees and  actually below the dotted line means  that it had less bees so we see this  kind of goldilocks a zone with moderate  amounts of natural habitat in the  surrounding landscape predicting farms  that will do the best in terms of  increasing pollination services when  they add a wildflower strip so some  benefit but only at this sort of sweet  spot of farms and very likely that's  because in farms with very little  natural habitat there's just not a  sufficient pollinator population and to  recruit to that relatively small  wildflower planting  and at farms where there's a lot of  natural habitat adding a small  wildflower planting is a drop in the  bucket they already had sufficient  pollination services  to begin with we saw um that even at  those farms where there was a positive  effect it took about three years for us  to start to see that effect so  definitely a long-term investment and  something to think about in terms of how  you're rotating  your plantings if you're establishing  one of these  wildflower habitats that's going to take  three years before you start to see an  effect  in terms of pests we actually do see  evidence that there can be a spillover  of pests so there was sort of a  goldilocks zone again here in the middle  but no area where there was truly a  substantial benefit of adding a  wildflower planting in terms of a  reduction in pest pressure  and in some of these areas here we  actually saw  substantial increases in pest pressure  at those sites some of that  has to do with the fact that our key  pests like the tarnish plant bug  really like  many of the plants that showed up so  many of the plants here with asterisks  are ones that we planted but some of  them for example like a ridgeron that  daisy fleabane  that we saw earlier those  uh volunteered within our wildflower  plantings and they supported a lot of  tarnished plant bug but not a whole lot  of bees were using those compared to  some of the other species that we  intentionally planted into our plantings  it also was a little variable depending  on what our herbivore taxa was so  tarnished plant bugs which were the main  thing causing um damage in terms of  insects to our crop we did see a little  bit of an increase in tarnish plant bugs  at sites that had a wildflower strip  compared to controls  but  other pests like strawberry sap beetle  and other herbivores like aphids or  spittle bugs tended to be a little bit  lower and that's work that um one of the  undergraduates who worked with us  through the summer scholars program  ellie mccabe who's pictured here  spearheaded that project  so overall takeaways um from today are  that we are seeing um substantial  variation in weed pressure across farms  using some of those physical barriers  like plastic mulches or weed fabrics can  be effective although  there are certainly costs and things to  think about in terms of how you manage  those across their life cycle  grasses tend to be one of the most  impactful  weeds within the system but there are  other flowering plants that may be  providing some beneficial services in  terms of supporting pollinators or  natural enemies  overall in terms of pollination those  wild bees are really important for  providing pollination services on farms  and honeybees  and even  managed bumblebee colonies which are  quite expensive to purchase are probably  not going to be very effective  pollinators and if anything they're just  out there pollinating your flowering  weed populations  and in terms of pest management  wildflower plantings can if uh not very  carefully managed result in spillover of  pests so it's really important to make  sure that the seed mixes that you're  getting or even the volunteer plants  like fleabane are not  coming up and providing these really  nice alternative hosts for  potential pests within the system  so i'm incredibly grateful to all of the  growers some of which are pictured here  many of whom i think are on the call  today who allowed me over  many years to come and collect some of  this data on your farms i also have to  thank all of the labs that i've  collaborated with over the years and  have contributed to the project of  our funders as well and if there's time  i think we're near the end but i'm happy  to take any questions  laura we can't hear you  uh no i'm not sure what's gone wrong  hi i'll um  laura  i'm not sure what's going on but i can  take over uh we do have some time for  questions session 4 is going to start at  3 45 pm  uh so thank you so much heather for this  talk this was super interesting  um  and  jim is asking  how might you convince growers to stop  using bumblebee quads for pollination  because money  oh go ahead yeah um so i would say of  course if you see on your own farm if  you're  purchasing these and they are working  for you  that's great  one piece of data that i think was  really really impactful for me which i  didn't share earlier  is this one so we worked in  not just strawberry for the study but we  also surveyed in raspberry and tomato  and we saw very few bumble bees so there  was no bumble bees in our control plots  very few bumblebees visiting strawberry  across all the farms we surveyed that  had these quads  um and importantly in later flowering  crops especially for tomato we actually  saw that there were fewer bumblebees at  states where we had put a quad compared  to sites that don't and the main reason  for that just some work spearheaded by  another student i worked with olivia  miller is that these quads are killing  large numbers of wild bumblebee queens  that enter through these um hive  entrances when they do not have  queen excluders on them  i have a little video of that you can  see there's  queens that are wild that's a wild queen  trying to get into one of these quads if  you do not have an excluder so there's  an excluder on there that keeps her from  getting in you can easily kill 20 or 30  wild bumblebee queens and that's a  colony then that no longer establishes  and produces workers that pollinate all  of your other crops for the rest of the  season so that's one piece of data that  really convinced me  why it's probably not a good idea to  invest in these even if they were  increasing your pollination in  strawberry  oh that just breaks my heart thank you  so much for sharing that video  um i also i do need to remind everyone  that if you want dec credits  you need to fill out the survey that's  in the chat one more time we will not be  offering the ec credits in the fourth  session so this is your last chance  um  and oh oh here's a good question um  chuck is asking they see a lot of  bumblebees pollinating and blueberries  what do you think about that  so blueberries could actually be a  crop system where putting quads in  may be effective i've definitely seen a  lot of bumblebees as well pollinating  blueberries  they're very effective blueberry  pollinators because bumblebees do that  buzz pollination activity so most of the  pollination needs for blueberry as long  as there's sufficient natural habitat  around can be met by the wild bee  populations there's some great work out  of university of vermont that's showing  that supplementing with bumblebees only  very rarely  gives a positive impact although more so  than it does for strawberry but i would  still be quite worried about these  long-term population impacts of putting  these quads out  particularly when they do not have a  excluder on them which is not currently  how companies you'd have to ask  especially to get those excluders  another question that i have for you is  one time i was at a talk  um and this entomologist researcher says  that  on farms that spray pesticides and  insecticides  and they have um  they have these field borders  of wildflowers they actually found the  highest concentrations of pesticides in  those  borders which of course isn't  it's a little bit counterintuitive  so i'm wondering  where  do you think would be a good place to  uh encourage natural vegetation  in a farm system  yeah i think that's a really tricky  question i also get that a lot from  folks who are doing a lot of under  canopy management so blueberry growers  and also orchard folks who want to  figure out like if i'm spraying should i  mow down all of the dandelions and  clovers that are in my field and i think  that is a good practice to do if you're  spraying anything that has high toxicity  for beneficial insects  because they can always forage elsewhere  to find some of those pollen and nectar  resources they need but if they forage  on something toxic and they die then  you know you're out of luck in terms of  your pollinators  um  not having  your planting right immediately at the  edge but with some buffer is probably a  good idea so often in our plantings they  would be a couple meters away  from our experimental planting maybe  there's a laneway that's going in  between  the field which is also really helpful  for getting cultivation or harvesting  equipment in and out of the field as  well  thank you  and i see a couple folks in the chat are  saying that um  they see wild bumblebees on their farm  which makes me happy and sea square is  confirming that wild bees are better  than bumble bees  is that correct  um so in the case of strawberry  pollination that is correct we don't see  so there are queen bumblebees that are  flying around during strawberry bloom i  occasionally see a wild queen so you can  tell they're wild because they're really  big whereas the workers in the colonies  that you buy are much smaller compared  to the wild queens that are out they  don't tend to visit strawberry it's  mostly this other suite of really  diverse and much smaller pollinators  that are visiting strawberry and  providing services  so would these be like those tiny  uh shiny green ones exactly yeah so some  of the pictures i showed earlier  um show you just the absolutely gorgeous  diversity  of different bees that we've been seeing  in our surveys so shiny metallic green  midnight blue  red with stripes they're they're very  beautiful  that's incredible  um oh great a question from brian young  um  he says that oh it's a comet we stopped  using quads five or so years ago and we  see many more wild bees carpenter bees  small bees  that's fantastic  yeah and we've actually one thing that  was wild to see in those bumblebee  colonies um the reason why wild queens  are going in there is because it's part  of their natural behavior to try to  usurp or to steal the colonies of other  wild queens that have already  established they basically get all the  benefits of having a nest without having  to do all the work of starting that nest  and we see other bees as well who are  also trying to get in so other bumblebee  species including species that we're  really worried about in terms of their  population declines over time but i've  also seen carpenter bees in those nests  i've seen tons of honeybees that are  going in there and trying to probably  steal nectar or other resources from the  nest we see all kinds of wild things  showing up in these managed bumblebee  colonies  that's fascinating  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu


